## Proof

We determine all real $p$ for which $\displaystyle S(p)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\sin(n^p)$ converges.

A necessary condition for convergence of any series is that its terms tend to $0$. We split into cases.

### Case 1: $p<0$

Here $n^p\to 0^{+}$, so $\sin(n^p)\sim n^p$ (since $\sin x/x\to 1$ as $x\to 0$), and both quantities are positive for all large $n$. By the limit comparison test, $S(p)$ converges if and only if $\sum n^p=\sum n^{-|p|}$ converges, which happens exactly when $|p|>1$, i.e. **$p<-1$**.

- If $p<-1$: $\sum n^p$ converges (p-series with exponent $>1$), so $S(p)$ converges.
- If $-1\le p<0$: $\sum n^p$ diverges, and since $\sin(n^p)>0$ eventually with $\sin(n^p)/n^p\to 1$, $S(p)$ diverges (to $+\infty$).

### Case 2: $p=0$

Then $n^p=1$ for every $n$, so the series is $\sum\sin(1)$, a nonzero constant series. **Diverges.**

### Case 3: $p>0$

We show the terms $\sin(n^p)$ do **not** tend to $0$, which forces divergence.

We use the classical equidistribution theorem of **Weyl–van der Corput**:

> **Theorem.** For every real $p>0$ and every irrational $\alpha$, the sequence $\{\alpha\, n^p\}_{n\ge 1}$ is uniformly distributed modulo $1$.

(For integer $p\ge 1$ this is Weyl's polynomial equidistribution theorem, requiring an irrational coefficient; for non-integer $p>0$ it follows from van der Corput's exponential-sum estimates. Both apply here.)

Take $\alpha=\dfrac{1}{2\pi}$, which is irrational (since $\pi$ is irrational). Then $\left\{\dfrac{n^p}{2\pi}\right\}$ is uniformly distributed modulo $1$, equivalently $n^p\bmod 2\pi$ is uniformly distributed in $[0,2\pi)$.

Uniform distribution implies the sequence $\{n^p\bmod 2\pi\}$ is dense in $[0,2\pi)$; in particular it visits the interval $(\pi/3,\,2\pi/3)$ infinitely often. On this interval $\sin x\ge \sin(\pi/3)=\sqrt 3/2>0$. Hence there are infinitely many $n$ with $\sin(n^p)\ge\sqrt 3/2$, so $\sin(n^p)\not\to 0$.

Therefore the necessary condition for convergence fails, and **$S(p)$ diverges for every $p>0$**.

### Conclusion

Combining the three cases:

$$\boxed{\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\sin(n^p)\ \text{converges}\ \iff\ p<-1.}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
